# Updates — releases published by nodes, signed

A node publishes **its own code** as one signed package; it announces that it
has it, and hands it over to whoever asks. A node that received it keeps it and
becomes somewhere else to ask. If the operator pinned that publisher's key, the
release can be installed — optionally on its own.

Finding one is a **question**, not a list: you type part of a name, or you open
a node's details and see what that machine offers. What comes back is a signed
record carrying its publisher's key — so pinning is a confirmation of something
that arrived with the thing it signed, rather than a hex string copied from a
channel nobody could vouch for.

Code: [`src/core_release.py`](../../src/core_release.py) (the format, the pins,
the catalogue), [`src/pkg_dir.py`](../../src/pkg_dir.py) (the directory: signed
records, the keys they are filed under, the source digest),
[`src/subscriptions.py`](../../src/subscriptions.py) (what this node watches),
the release and package sections of [`src/node.py`](../../src/node.py)
(publishing, gossip, lookup, installing), [`src/updater.py`](../../src/updater.py)
(the tree swap, shared with the GitHub path).

## Why

Downloading an update from a web host makes one company's account the root of
trust for every node on the mesh. A mesh that carries sensitive traffic through
hostile territory and then takes its own code from a single web service has a
front door it does not control.

So: no host in the way. The bytes travel the same DHT that already carries app
packages, and the only thing that says "this is a real release" is a signature
from a key the operator chose to accept.

The GitHub path is still there ([`../Setup/guide`](../Setup/guide)) — it is how
a node with no peers yet gets its first version. It is no longer the only way.
What it looks at is one setting: the published releases by default, or
`src/version.py` at the branch `update_branch` names. Neither is signed for this
node, which is the whole point of what follows.

## Publishing costs nothing

Publishing is **signing and announcing**. The tree is packed into one
deterministic `tar.gz`, hashed, and a descriptor naming that hash is signed and
gossiped. No network at all: a node with no peers can publish, and 1.8 MB of
source becomes about 0.5 MB that only moves when somebody wants it.

The first cut of this pushed the tree onto the DHT as ~120 content-addressed
chunks, each costing a Kademlia lookup **at publish time**. That is a hundred
round trips paid up front, for nodes that may never ask — minutes of an operator
watching a spinner on a real mesh. Measured on this repository: **119 lookups
before, 0 now, 0.18 s to publish.**

## The three separate things

They are deliberately not merged, and each is worth naming:

| | What it proves | What it does not |
|---|---|---|
| **the package** (SHA-256) | these are exactly the bytes the descriptor names | who made them |
| **the descriptor** (ML-DSA signature) | *this publisher* published *those bytes*, at *that time* | that you want their code |
| **the pin** (an operator's decision) | this node accepts that publisher | anything about the content |

The hash means a relay can neither substitute nor corrupt a package: whoever
hands the bytes over, they either match what was signed or they are dropped. The
signature is what makes "who published this" a question with an answer. And the
pin is the only one of the three that cannot arrive over the network.

## The descriptor

```
{v, version, size, sha256, publisher, ts, notes, sig}
```

- `size` / `sha256` — the package: how many bytes to expect, and what they must
  hash to. A release is named by that hash, so two nodes holding the same
  release agree on what to ask each other for.
- `publisher` — the publisher's ML-DSA public key. The **publisher id** is
  `sha256(key)[:20]`, derived and never carried: an id that does not follow from
  the key presented is not a mismatch to resolve, it is a forgery.
- `ts` — the signed publish time; the catalogue keeps the highest per publisher.
- `sig` — ML-DSA-65 over `nmesh-core-release-v1 ‖ <the fields above>`.

**The signing domain is its own.** The same identity key signs certificates,
handshakes, app releases, directory claims and app-auth assertions; a shared
domain would let one be replayed as another. An app release descriptor is not a
core release and will not parse as one — there is a test for exactly that.

### The version cannot lie

The `version` in the descriptor is checked against `__version__` in
`src/version.py` **inside the package** — when the release is built, and again
after it is fetched, before anything touches disk. A release that announces
0.9.6 and carries something else is refused rather than unpacked. Without that
check the signed version would be decoration.

## Publishing

Console → **Settings → Updates → Publish this node's code**, or:

```python
info = await node.publish_release(notes="what changed")
# {"version", "release_id", "publisher_id", "files", "bytes"}
```

The tree that is installed here is read (`src`, `scripts`, `start.sh`,
`install.sh`, `requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `Docs`, `docker`,
`README.md`, `CLAUDE.md` — the same set the updater swaps in), packed and
signed. The archive is **deterministic** — sorted paths, no mtimes, no uid/gid,
a fixed mode, and gzip's own header timestamp pinned — so the same tree packs to
the same bytes on any machine: a rebuild is not a new release, and two
publishers of the same source agree on the hash. What is **not** carried: the node's state, its virtualenv, the
liboqs build, `__pycache__`, and **symlinks** — a release is regular files, and
a link is a way of pointing the extraction somewhere it was never meant to
write.

The publisher key to hand out is shown on that same card. Give it to people over
a channel you trust: **anyone who can change that string on its way to them can
ship them code.**

The publisher keeps the package (`<data>/packages/`) so it has something to
hand over. Everything else happens on demand.

Publishing also **files a record in the package directory**: a small signed
pointer at the descriptor, filed under this key's publisher id and under the
name's prefixes. That is what makes the release findable by somebody who has
never heard of the key. It costs no lookup at publish time either — the record
is signed and the descriptor kept locally; the directory sweep is what
replicates both, which is where work that needs the network belongs.

## Finding a package

Console → **Settings → Updates → From the mesh**, or **Apps → Find an app**, or
any node's details page. Three entry points, one view
([`src/webassets/packages.py`](../../src/webassets/packages.py)), and one shape
of answer.

Typing searches what this node already holds — instant and free, so a field can
answer as somebody types. **Ask the network** spends one bounded round of
directory queries, which is why it is a button. A node's details page asks the
other half of the question: *does this machine publish a version, or does it
simply say which one it runs?*

Opening a record shows a page of its own (`/package#<id>`):

- **what it is and whose it is** — name, version, publisher, and whether this is
  a publication or a recommendation;
- **what it says about itself** — the few signed lines the publisher wrote. It is
  the only description anybody has before fetching, so it is shown before any
  button;
- **what agrees with it** — how many publishers have signed a package carrying
  the same *code*, documentation excluded;
- **what you can do** — download it to open by hand, install it, watch it. In
  that order, because reading before running is the whole argument for offering
  a download at all.

## Pinning a publisher

From the package that key signed: one press and a confirmation naming the id
that will be pinned. The key came inside the record and was checked against the
signature it made, so there is nothing to copy across and nothing to get wrong —
which was the point of removing the paste-a-hex-key form. Whether their releases
install automatically is a separate tick in the same dialog, and it starts
unticked: trusting a publisher and handing them a scheduled restart are two
decisions.

A **recommendation** cannot be pinned. It names somebody else's bytes, and
pinning the machine that agreed with a release would hand this node to whoever
agreed — which is not what agreeing means. The console says so and points at the
publisher it references.

Pinning is still the only way a key enters that list — no packet, no peer, no app
can add one. The file (`<data>/publishers.json`, mode 0600) holds public keys and
two booleans; a corrupt or doctored file yields **no** trusted publisher rather
than a guess. An entry whose id does not follow from its key is dropped, so a
tampered file cannot slip a publisher in under a familiar id.

Failing closed there costs an operator one re-pin. Failing open costs them the
machine.

### Two flags, two different statements

A pinned key carries **`auto`** and **`endorsed`**, and neither implies the
other:

- **`auto`** — "this one key may replace my code without asking me". Fast, and
  exactly as strong as that key: whoever holds it holds the machine.
- **`endorsed`** — "this key's word counts towards a quorum". Much weaker alone,
  and it is the route for code from parties none of whom you would hand the
  machine to individually.

## Installing from people you do not individually trust

## Publishing without being asked

`release_auto_publish` in `nmesh.conf` (off, and off is the default) makes a
node offer **its own running version** to the mesh whenever that version
changes — which is what turns a node that updated itself into one that also
carries the update onward, instead of sitting on it until somebody presses a
button.

Three things bound it, and the third is why the default is what it is:

- **Once per version.** The pass asks whether *our* key already offers this
  exact version, so a restart, a re-announce or a second sweep publish nothing.
  A tree that fails to publish is not retried for an hour: reading and hashing
  it is not free, and it will not become publishable because we asked again a
  second later.
- **It announces, it does not push.** Publishing signs a descriptor and says so;
  the package moves only when somebody asks. Nobody installs it who has not
  separately pinned this node's key, and pinning is always a human act.
- **It signs with the node identity.** That key is unlocked for as long as the
  node runs, because handshakes need it — which is exactly why
  `publish_release` prefers a separate publisher key when a person is there to
  unlock one. Unattended, there is nobody to type a passphrase, so this uses the
  identity. Turning it on says "whoever holds this machine may offer its code to
  everyone who pinned it". That is already true of anyone who can press the
  button; the difference is that this is a standing permission rather than a
  decision, and standing permissions deserve to be opted into.

## Installing without being asked

`release_quorum` in `nmesh.conf` (0 = off, and off is the default) says how many
**endorsed publishers must independently sign the same `(version, sha256)`**
before a release may install itself unattended.

**Serving is not vouching**, and this is the distinction the obvious design gets
wrong. Mirroring a package is free, and content addressing already makes the
bytes safe to fetch from anyone — a thousand mirrors say nothing that one does
not. What costs an attacker something is a second *key* put behind the same
content. So the quorum counts signatures, never servers, and the same key
signing twice is one party.

Endorsement is manual, one key at a time. That is the whole defence against the
obvious attack: minting two hundred publishers is as cheap as minting two
hundred identities, but a quorum made of keys a human chose cannot be reached by
creating anything — only by compromising keys that were already chosen.

### A quorum has to be independent to be a quorum

Counting to K is not enough if the K are one party. A group of publishers signed
— not necessarily directly, but through however many hands — by a single issuer
is one family wearing several hats, however many of them an operator ticked; and
a subtree grown in order to have somewhere to publish from is exactly what that
looks like from outside.

So before a quorum counts, the endorsed signers are traced through the
certificate store, which has always been a graph of who vouched for whom and had
never been queried as one (`CertStore.ancestors` / `shared_ancestor`). If more
than half of them descend from one issuer **below a root**, the quorum is
refused and the reason names the issuer.

Two deliberate limits on this. A network's root is shared by everybody in it, so
roots are excluded — a common root must never read as a common family. And a
publisher key that does not publish from its node cannot be placed in the graph
at all; such a signer is counted as **independent**, which errs towards
installing rather than refusing on a suspicion we cannot support.

It is not an accusation. A family is not a conspiracy, and nothing here is held
against anybody: the answer is simply to stop counting them as several.

Two further refusals apply to **both** routes, because they say the *code* is in
question rather than the messenger:

- somebody claims this version with **different content** — a fork, or somebody's
  own build wearing a version everyone recognises;
- the publisher we would install from **contradicts itself**, having signed two
  different programs under one version number.

The first accuses nobody: two honest publishers can disagree by accident, and
the answer to that is the same as to an attack — stop, and let a human look. The
second does accuse, and is the one refusal here entitled to. Refusing an update
is recoverable; installing a hostile one is not.

### A publisher that contradicts itself

No accident makes one key sign one version twice with different bytes. The
catalogue keeps the two descriptors as a pair (`src/equivocation.py`), and the
pair is **checkable by anyone**: forging either half needs the publisher's own
private key, so whoever shows it contributes nothing but transport. It is the
only report on this mesh that stands up without the messenger's honesty in it —
see [`../Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md#equivocation-the-one-report-that-needs-no-trusted-messenger).
The pair is kept locally and is not gossiped yet — that is the next step, and it
needs the bounding a 20 kB broadcast deserves before it is built.

The detection runs **before** the anti-rollback drop, deliberately: a catalogue
that dropped the older descriptor first would never see the pair, and showing
the older half second is precisely how a publisher would arrange that.

What it costs them here is narrow. Nothing installs itself from that key any
more, the row on the Updates page carries a **contradicts itself** badge, and
the node's activity feed says so. A human may still install by hand — they are
told first. No standing moves and nothing is cut off: this is about one key
having lied once, not about how that node behaves on the wire.

The console shows, per release, how many keys signed that exact content, how
many of them are endorsed, whether anyone disputes the version, whether the
publisher contradicted itself, and in one sentence why it would or would not
install itself.

## Signing with a key that is not in memory

The node's identity key has to stay loaded — it signs handshakes, certificates
and directory claims continuously. A **publisher** key is the opposite case: it
is used a few times a year and it decides what code every node pinning it will
run. Sharing one key for both made the most consequential secret in the project
also the most exposed.

`src/publisher_key.py` keeps a publisher key on disk encrypted under a
passphrase (scrypt → AES-256-GCM), and `publish_release(key_path=…,
passphrase=…)` unlocks it for exactly as long as it takes to sign, then gives
the liboqs context back. Nothing in the running node holds it.

The whole header — salt and scrypt cost parameters included — is the AEAD's
associated data, so an attacker who can edit the file cannot lower the
derivation cost and hand it back to be opened cheaply. The cost parameters are
*stored*, never assumed: a file written today must still open in five years on a
build whose defaults have moved. The public half is readable without the
passphrase, because "which key is this" is a public question.

What the passphrase buys is the case that actually happens: the file is copied —
a backup, a stolen laptop, a misconfigured share. It buys nothing against an
attacker present *while you publish*; at that moment the key is in memory by
necessity, and an operator who needs that property needs a machine that is not
the node.

## Handing that key to somebody else

`src/key_share.py`. A team publishing one thing under one identity needs the
key on more than one machine, and the way that happens otherwise is a copy over
a chat window — which is the worst version of it: no proof the sender holds
what they offer, no proof the recipient is who the sender meant, and a
passphrase typed in the same window as the file.

Say the cost first, because it is real: **sharing a signing key means a
compromise is the project, not one signer**. The alternative exists and is
already built — each person keeps their own key, consumers pin several and set
`release_quorum` over `endorsed` ones. Sharing is right when consumers should
not have to track who is on the team this year, and wrong the moment "one more
person" becomes routine. Nothing here can tell those apart; what it does is
make each act deliberate on both sides and leave a record of who handed what to
whom.

### Three messages, and why the middle one is the design

```
OFFER    sender    → recipient   "I hold key P and I offer it to you"
ACCEPT   recipient → sender      "I want it — seal it to this KEM key"
GRANT    sender    → recipient   the secret, sealed so only they can open it
```

There is no long-term encryption key to seal a secret to: a node's identity is
ML-DSA, a *signing* key, and the ML-KEM keys it uses are ephemeral per link. So
the recipient has to produce one, and it produces one **only when a human
accepted**. Consent is therefore structural rather than checked — with no
acceptance there is nothing to seal to, and the secret cannot leave the
sender's machine even by mistake.

### What each signature is for

- The **offer is signed by the publisher key itself**, not by the node. That
  proves the sender actually holds what they are offering; otherwise anybody
  could offer a key they do not have and harvest acceptances. Both node ids are
  inside the signature, so the reply goes where the *signer* said rather than to
  whatever `src_id` a relay wrote on the packet.
- The **acceptance is signed by the recipient's node identity**, over the KEM
  public key, and the parser re-derives the node id from the key inside it
  (`node_id_of(pub) == from_id`). That is what stops a relay substituting its own
  KEM key and reading the grant: only the node the offer named can produce an
  acceptance the sender acts on.
- The **grant carries no signature**, and does not need one. It is sealed to the
  accepted KEM key (`HKDF(shared, info=domain:grant:offer_id)` → AES-256-GCM,
  with both node ids and the publisher public key as associated data), and what
  proves it genuine is arithmetic: the recipient checks the delivered secret
  against the public half **from the offer** via `CryptoIdentity.from_pair`. A
  forged or tampered grant yields a pair that does not match itself and is
  refused — the same check `publisher_key.load` already makes.

### What it does not hide

The sender must unlock the key to sign the offer and to seal the grant, so the
secret sits in that process's memory for the length of the handshake. That is
unavoidable — you cannot send what you have not unlocked. It is *bounded*
instead: the sender forgets it when the grant goes out, or when the offer
expires unanswered (`OFFER_TTL`, 5 minutes), whichever comes first.

The recipient chooses **its own** passphrase. A shared one would make the weaker
of the two machines the security of both, and the whole point of
`publisher_key.py` is that the file is useless to whoever copies it.

### Where the keys live

`publisher_key.KeyStore` (under `<state>/publisher-keys/`) holds the encrypted
files this node can sign with, whether created here or received. The id is
always **re-derived from the file's own public half** on read, never taken from
the index: a tampered index can lose a key or mislabel it, but it cannot make
the node sign under an identity the file does not carry. Signing picks one by id
in the publish form; the file's passphrase is asked for at that moment and kept
nowhere.

Both directions are visible in **Settings → Updates**: the keys held here, the
offers made from here and still standing, and the offers waiting for an answer.
Accepting asks for a passphrase and a confirmation; refusing is one click and
tells the sender nothing beyond the offer lapsing.

## The package directory

`src/pkg_dir.py`. Same shape as the pseudo directory and deliberately so: a
**self-authenticating record**, safe to accept from a stranger, cache and
re-serve, worth no authority at all.

```
record = version ‖ kind ‖ flags ‖ ts ‖ pubkey ‖ name ‖ pkg_version
         ‖ notes ‖ ref ‖ src ‖ ML-DSA signature
signed over  "nmesh-package-dir-v1" ‖ publisher_id ‖ kind ‖ flags ‖ ts
             ‖ name ‖ pkg_version ‖ notes ‖ ref ‖ src
```

| Field | What it is |
|---|---|
| `kind` | `core` (the node's own code) or `app` (a third-party application) |
| `flags` | `FLAG_RECOMMEND`: the signer is not the author — "this is the release id I run" |
| `name` | the displayed name, in the same canonical form a pseudo takes |
| `notes` | the few lines shown before anything is fetched, ≤ `MAX_NOTES` |
| `ref` | the **DHT content key** of the signed descriptor this points at |
| `src` | a digest over the package's code with its documentation left out |

Three properties, and together they are the security argument:

- **the publisher id is derived from the pubkey inside the record**, and the
  signature is checked under that same key — a record can only ever say what its
  own author publishes. Nobody can file a package against somebody else's
  identity, which is what makes a directory of strangers usable at all;
- **the keys are derived, never declared.** A reader recomputes them from the
  name inside the signed record, so a publisher cannot file itself under a name
  it did not sign;
- **`ts` only moves forward** per (publisher, kind, name), so a relay replaying
  an old record cannot walk anybody back to a stale version.

A publisher id is `sha256(pubkey)[:20]`, built exactly like a node id — so a node
signing with its own identity publishes **under its own id**, which is what lets
a details page ask "what does this machine offer?" with nothing but the id
already on the screen. A node that signs with a separate publisher key
(`publisher_key.py`) is reached through a **pairing**, below: the record names
the key, because that is what the signature proves.

### Pairing a detached key with the machine that uses it

A publisher key is deliberately not a node identity: it decides what everybody
pinning it will run, it is used a few times a year, and it has no business
sharing the key a node keeps unlocked to sign handshakes. But a record can only
name its own signer, so nothing tied that key to the machine, and an operator
looking at a node could not get from it to the code it publishes.

A pairing closes that, and it takes **two halves**, because one would be
hearsay:

```
node N signs   "P publishes for me"   → filed under N's key
key  P signs   "N is my node"         → filed under P's key
```

Each half can only name its own author as the signer, exactly like every other
record here. A reader follows the link only when it holds **both and each names
the other** — `PairingBook.confirmed`. With one half alone, a node could put a
stranger's packages on its own page; that grants no privilege (the package still
shows its real publisher, and pinning still pins *that* publisher) but it is one
node's word about another, believed, which is the shape the charter forbids.

Both halves are signed at publish time: the publisher's while its key is open —
the only moment it is — and the node's because it costs nothing. They travel on
the same directory plane as package records, filed under the same keys, and a
reader tries both gates on each blob.

A key may be paired with several machines (one publisher key across a fleet),
bounded by `_MAX_PAIRINGS_PER_KEY`; the newest half per (signer, counterpart)
wins, so a replayed old one cannot undo a party's newer word.

### Two keys, two questions

```
sha256(domain : "pub:"  ‖ publisher_id)[:20]     what does this key offer?
sha256(domain : "name:" ‖ term)[:20]             who offers something called this?
```

`term` runs over the whole folded name and each word's prefixes
(`pseudo.key_terms`, shared with the pseudo directory because a name is a name).
So typing three letters lands on a key the package was already filed under.

### The source digest — "am I looking at the official version?"

`pkg_dir.source_digest` hashes the package's files **excluding its
documentation**: `README*`, `Docs/`, `CHANGELOG`, `LICENSE`, `CLAUDE.md` and the
like. Nothing in that set is executed by anything, which is the whole test for
what may be excluded — a file that runs must change the digest, or "these
parties agree on the code" would be a sentence about nothing.

Two publishers who build the same source with different release notes therefore
ship different bytes and carry the same `src`. The digest travels in the record,
so the question is answerable **before anything is downloaded**.

It never replaces the content hash. What gets installed is still verified byte
for byte against the descriptor the operator chose; `src` only decides whether
enough independent parties agree on the code.

## Watching a package

Turn on **Watch for new versions** on a package's page. A subscription names one
publisher's offering of one package
([`src/subscriptions.py`](../../src/subscriptions.py), `<data>/subscriptions.json`,
mode 0600), and carries two things:

- **Install them without asking** — this subscription may install on its own,
  subject to every gate below;
- **Publishers that must agree** — the quorum. Watch several publishers of the
  same package and ask for two, and an automatic install waits until two of the
  ones *you watch* have signed the same `src`. Ask for one, which is the default,
  and it installs whatever that publisher offers.

Counted over publishers this operator chose, never over everybody who signed
something: a signature from somebody nobody subscribed to is a party an attacker
can mint. Same argument as the endorsed quorum next door, applied to a set the
operator built one package at a time.

A quorum only ever **withholds**. It cannot authorise an install the publisher
pins would refuse — a core release still needs its publisher pinned, still has
to be strictly newer, and still has to verify byte for byte.

The sweep also asks the directory what each subscribed publisher offers now
(`_subscription_pass`), which is the half an announcement cannot cover: a
publisher whose announce never reached us, or one we met long after it
published.

## When this node goes looking

Three settings in `nmesh.conf`, all editable from **Settings → Configuration**:

| Setting | Default | What it decides |
|---|---|---|
| `update_check_minutes` | 5 | minutes between sweeps; **0 = never go looking** |
| `update_when_active` | off | only sweep while somebody is using this node (a console, an app, a call) |
| `recommend_version` | off | publish which release id this node runs, so nodes watching it can see the version it settled on |

An announcement still wakes the node whatever these say. What they control is
the node going to look **on its own**, which on a phone is where the battery
goes: an hourly sweep that skips while the screen is off is a different machine
from one checking every five minutes in a pocket.

`recommend_version` publishes a record with `FLAG_RECOMMEND`: it points at the
release id this node runs and carries the **source digest of the tree actually
running here**. That is a second party saying "this code is what I am
executing" — the one thing in a supply chain an attacker cannot obtain by
minting identities. It publishes no code and authorises nothing; whoever reads
it decides what their own subscriptions make of it. Computed once per version,
because reading and hashing the tree is not free and the answer cannot change
while the version does not.

## What the mesh offers

`RELEASE_ANNOUNCE` (0x1E) carries a descriptor between **direct authenticated**
peers, re-stamped at each hop, exactly like the app catalogue's gossip
([`../AppStore/guide`](../AppStore/guide)):

1. verify the signature — reject by default;
2. keep it only if the publisher is unknown to us or the `ts` is strictly higher
   (anti-rollback: replaying an old signed release cannot walk a node back);
3. re-gossip **only when our view changed**, which is what makes the epidemic
   terminate.

A freshly authenticated peer is caught up on what we hold, so a node that joins
learns about releases published before it arrived.

**A release from a publisher nobody here pinned is still carried and relayed.**
Refusing to relay what we would not install ourselves would break discovery for
every other operator. It is shown flagged `unpinned`, and offers no action.

The catalogue is bounded (one entry per publisher, `MAX_CATALOG`) and reserves
room for pinned publishers: when it is full, a trusted newcomer evicts the
oldest untrusted entry and an untrusted newcomer is simply refused. A flood of
strangers cannot push out the publisher you actually rely on.

Announces are rate-limited per ingress link (`_RELEASE_RATE_MAX` per window):
verifying an ML-DSA signature is work, and a peer does not get to ask for it
without end.

## Moving the package

`RELEASE_ANNOUNCE` carries `have(1) ‖ descriptor`: the byte says whether the
node **sending it** holds the package, re-answered at every hop, because the
answer is about the sender and not about the release. A node that hears it
remembers who claimed to have what — a hint only, bounded on both axes, and one
that costs at most a wasted request if it is a lie, because the hash is what
decides.

Fetching then asks the holders first and the peers it has after them — and
**never the publisher**:

> A release is bytes and a signature. The SHA-256 was signed before any of this
> started, so who serves the bytes cannot matter, and nothing in the fetch path
> is allowed to name one machine as *the* source.

That last rule is newer than the code around it. The fetch used to end at
`NodeID(publisher_id(key))`, which is wrong twice over. A publisher key is not a
machine: it can be detached from every node, and since the handover it can be
held by several at once — so that id names nobody, and a fetch spent a full
routed lookup finding out. And on the ordinary path, where the signer did use
its node identity, it made that one machine the last resort for the whole
network: publish once, serve for ever, and go offline and your release is gone.
Both are the same mistake, looking at the publisher instead of at the signature.

So the list is: whoever said they hold it (the `have` byte, from an
authenticated link — the only claim of actually having the bytes), then peers
that speak the plane at all, bounded by `_RELEASE_ASK_MAX`. A node that does not
have it costs one round trip and says nothing, which is cheaper than assuming.


- `RELEASE_FETCH` — `release_id(20) ‖ offset(4)`, routed, so a holder several
  hops away is reachable;
- `RELEASE_DATA` — `release_id(20) ‖ offset(4) ‖ slice`, up to
  `_RELEASE_SLICE` bytes.

A release is public code, signed: there is nothing to authorise in serving it
beyond not letting one peer pull without end, which is what
`_RELEASE_SERVE_MAX` per window does. A node that does not hold the package
stays silent rather than answering "no" — one less thing to forge.

The bytes are checked against the signed SHA-256 **before** anything is
unpacked. What comes back either matches or is dropped and the next source is
tried, so it does not matter who handed it over.

### Then it is a swarm

Whatever a node ends up holding, it keeps (`ReleaseStore`, bounded to
`MAX_HELD_PACKAGES`, on disk so a restart does not undo it) and starts
answering for. One publisher becomes a swarm as the release spreads, and a
publisher that goes offline stops being necessary — there is an integration
test for exactly that: the publisher is stopped, and a third node still gets
the release from the node that kept a copy.

A store that hands out what it was not given is worse than an empty one, so a
package is re-hashed when it is read back, not only when it is written.

## Installing

An install is addressed by **the descriptor in hand**, never by a publisher id
(`install_release_entry`). The catalogue is indexed by publisher and holds only
each key's newest signature, so resolving an install through it installed
*whatever that key had signed since* rather than the release on the screen — and
refused outright for a release the catalogue had never been gossiped, such as
one found by name in the directory. The publisher-keyed entry points remain for
what is genuinely a per-key question: the console's list of the keys this
operator pinned.

Three gates, in this order — the cheap ones first:

1. **the key that signed it is pinned** here — the signature, which is the only
   thing about a publisher that ever decides anything;
2. **the version is strictly newer** than the one running (`is_newer`), so a
   signed older release is not an install path;
3. **every byte verifies** against the signed SHA-256, and the tree's own
   version matches what the descriptor announced.

Only then does anything touch disk, and even then the previous tree is moved
aside and restored if the swap fails part-way — a half-replaced install is the
one outcome worth ruling out. Paths are checked one last time on the way in: a
package containing a path that reaches outside its own tree is **refused, not
sanitised**.

The node's state, its virtualenv and anything an operator left in the install
directory are never touched.

### Installing, then restarting

Replacing the tree changes nothing in a process that is already running: the
code is loaded. So the node has to come back, and there are **two ways** it can
(`updater.restart_plan`):

| Route | When | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| **the supervisor** | `NMESH_SERVICE_MANAGED=1` | the node stops properly and exits; systemd / runit / launchd starts it again. Preferred whenever it is available: the whole process image goes, along with any file descriptor, thread or C library state an update may have invalidated |
| **re-exec** | otherwise | `os.execv` replaces this process with a fresh interpreter on the same command line, keeping the pid, the session and the terminal. Not an exit: nothing outside has to cooperate |
| nothing | no interpreter to run, or the script the node was started from is gone | the node stays up and the console says why — leaving with nothing to catch it is worse than running yesterday's code |

**Re-exec is what Android needed.** Termux has no init a package can reach.
Without `termux-services` a phone installed an update and then sat on it until
somebody reopened the app and typed the command again — the update was applied
and the old code kept running, which is the failure mode hardest to notice. Every
descriptor Python opens is close-on-exec (PEP 446), so the listening sockets are
gone by the time the new image binds them; the node is stopped first anyway,
because a peer deserves a closed link rather than a reset one.

`install.sh` writes `Restart=always` into the systemd unit, `KeepAlive` into the
launchd plist, and `NMESH_SERVICE_MANAGED=1` into **every** unit it writes — the
runit service under Termux included, which is where it was missing: `runsv`
restarts the script whenever it exits, but the node could not tell, so it refused
to leave.

### Automatic installs

Three routes in, and they answer different questions:

- **a pinned key marked `auto`** — one key an operator handed a scheduled
  restart. Fast, and exactly as strong as that one key;
- **a quorum of endorsed publishers** over the same `(version, sha256)` — for
  code from parties none of whom you would hand the machine to alone;
- **a subscription marked "install without asking"**, once enough watched
  publishers agree on the same `src`. The same idea as the quorum, over a set
  the operator built one package at a time rather than one key at a time.

A machine provisioned from *Fleet → Discover & deploy* is handed its operator's
publishers with `auto` on ([`../Apps/fleet`](../Apps/fleet)) — a headless box has
nobody to sit at a console, so if it is not told at install time it accepts
nothing, for ever. Pinning and auto-installing stay two separate decisions.

A pass installs at most one release and remembers the release ids it has already
tried, so a failing one is not retried in a loop. Failures are recorded (visible
in the console) rather than raised: this loop dying would silently stop the
updates an operator asked for.

**What wakes it.** Waiting out a fixed tick was most of why automatic updates
felt like they did not work: a release could sit for five minutes, then install,
and then wait for a restart that never came. Now:

| | When |
|---|---|
| a release **arrives** from a publisher marked `auto` | the announce wakes the pass (`_RELEASE_SETTLE`, 3 s, so a peer catching us up with a burst costs one pass, not one per release) |
| the first pass after start | `_RELEASE_FIRST_TICK`, 20 s — enough to have peers, soon enough that a node rebooting into an update does not sit on the old version |
| the sweep | every `update_check_minutes` (5 by default, 0 = never), which covers a release we already held, a subscribed publisher that never announced at us, and any wake-up that went missing. `update_when_active` holds the sweep back while nobody is using the node — never the wake-up |

An announce from a publisher that is **not** pinned for automatic installation
wakes nothing: arrival must not be a lever a stranger can pull.

### The restart guard

Install-then-restart is a loop waiting to happen. The dangerous shape is not a
bad release — it is one that installs cleanly and **never becomes the running
version** (a tree the service manager does not start from, a swap something
shadows): the node would install it, restart, come back on the old code, and do
it again, for ever, on a machine nobody is watching. In-memory bookkeeping
cannot see that, because the process it would have to outlive is the one that
exits.

So the attempt is written to `<data>/autoinstall.json` (0600) **before** the node
leaves, and read back when it returns:

- the entry names the version the running one now **is** → it worked, forget it;
- it names a version this node is **not** running → say so in the release log
  ("did not take"), and count it;
- `MAX_AUTO_ATTEMPTS` (2) attempts without ever taking → **abandoned**, and the
  release is skipped from then on.

Installing by hand clears that record for the release: a deliberate act is a
fresh start, and an abandoned release must not be abandoned for ever. The
journal is bounded (`MAX_AUTO_JOURNAL`, 8), owner-only, and a corrupt one reads
as "nothing attempted" — fail-open in the harmless direction only, since the
count is what bounds it.

## Console API

| Method | Route | Role |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/releases` | what the mesh offers, who is pinned, what this node watches, when it looks, its own publisher key, and whether this install can update itself |
| POST | `/api/releases/publish` | `{notes, key_id?, passphrase?}` → sign the installed tree — with the node identity, or with a publisher key held here — announce it, file it in the directory |
| POST | `/api/releases/install` | `{publisher_id, confirm:true}` → fetch, verify, install |
| POST | `/api/releases/trust` | `{key, name?, auto?, endorsed?}` → pin a publisher by key |
| POST | `/api/releases/untrust` | `{publisher_id}` |
| POST | `/api/releases/auto` | `{publisher_id, auto}` — hand this one key a scheduled restart |
| POST | `/api/releases/endorse` | `{publisher_id, endorsed}` — let its signature count towards `release_quorum` |
| GET | `/api/packages?q=…[&wide=1]` | ask the directory for a name — `wide` also asks the network |
| GET | `/api/packages?node=<id>[&wide=1]` | what that node publishes or recommends |
| GET | `/api/packages/<id>[?fetch=1]` | one record; `fetch` also pulls the descriptor it points at |
| GET | `/api/packages/<id>/download` | the verified package, as an opaque `tar.gz` |
| POST | `/api/packages/install` | `{id, confirm:true}` → fetch, verify, install |
| POST | `/api/packages/trust` | `{id, confirm:true, auto?, endorsed?}` — pin the key **inside** that record |
| POST | `/api/packages/subscribe` | `{id, on, auto?, quorum?}` — watch it, or stop |
| GET | `/api/keys` | publisher keys held here, offers made from here, offers waiting for an answer |
| POST | `/api/keys/create` | `{passphrase, label?}` → a fresh publisher key, encrypted under it |
| POST | `/api/keys/import` | `{path, passphrase, label?}` → adopt an existing key file |
| POST | `/api/keys/offer` | `{node, key_id, passphrase, confirm:true, label?}` → offer it; nothing secret travels yet |
| POST | `/api/keys/accept` | `{offer_id, passphrase, confirm:true}` → ask for it, sealed to a fresh KEM key |
| POST | `/api/keys/refuse` | `{offer_id}` — drop it; the sender learns only that it lapsed |
| POST | `/api/keys/forget` | `{key_id, confirm:true}` — remove the file from this node |

Every route sits behind the console session, and every decision is made in the
node: each row arrives with its `trusted`, `attesters`, `agreeing` and
`subscription`, so the page never re-derives a rule that lives in Python.

## Bounds

| Constant | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `MAX_TREE_BYTES` | 64 MB | a release is source code, not a disk image — applied **while** unpacking, since decompression is where a small blob becomes a large one |
| `MAX_PACKAGE_BYTES` | 32 MB | the blob that carries it |
| `MAX_FILES` | 8192 | ditto |
| `MAX_HELD_PACKAGES` | 4 | helping the network is not unbounded storage |
| `_RELEASE_SLICE` | 48 KB | package bytes per packet |
| `_RELEASE_SERVE_MAX` | 64 / 10 s | slices one link may pull from us |
| `_RELEASE_SOURCES_MAX` | 8 | nodes remembered as holding a release |
| `_RELEASE_ASK_MAX` | 12 | nodes one fetch may ask before giving up |
| `MAX_NOTES_LEN` | 4000 | notes are shown in a page |
| `MAX_PUBLISHERS` | 32 | pinned keys an operator may hold |
| `MAX_CATALOG` | 64 | publishers tracked, trusted-first |
| `MAX_EQUIVOCATIONS` | 8 | publishers we keep a self-contradiction proof about, pinned-first |
| `_RELEASE_RATE_MAX` | 32 / 10 s | signature verifications one link may ask for |
| `_RELEASE_TRIED_MAX` | 32 | release ids remembered as already attempted |
| `_RELEASE_FIRST_TICK` | 20 s | before the first automatic pass after start |
| `_RELEASE_SETTLE` | 3 s | a burst of announces becomes one pass |
| `MAX_AUTO_ATTEMPTS` | 2 | automatic installs of a release that never takes |
| `MAX_AUTO_JOURNAL` | 8 | releases the restart guard remembers attempting |
| `pkg_dir._MAX_ENTRIES` | 512 | package records this node remembers at all |
| `pkg_dir._MAX_BOOK_BYTES` | 4 MB | …and their byte budget: a record carries an ML-DSA key and signature |
| `pkg_dir._MAX_PER_KEY` | 8 | records per directory key — a `PKG_FOUND` reply must fit one packet |
| `pkg_dir.MAX_NOTES` | 600 | the description shown before anything is fetched |
| `pkg_dir._MAX_PAIRINGS` | 256 | pairing halves this node remembers |
| `pkg_dir._MAX_PAIRING_BYTES` | 2 MB | …and their byte budget |
| `pkg_dir._MAX_PAIRINGS_PER_KEY` | 4 | machines one publisher key may be paired with |
| `_PKG_RATE_MAX` | 64 / 10 s | package records one link may push at us |
| `_PKG_OWN_MAX` | 32 | records this node signs and keeps re-filing |
| `MAX_SUBSCRIPTIONS` | 64 | packages this node watches |
| `MAX_QUORUM` | 8 | publishers a subscription may require to agree |
| `_DIR_PUBLISH_MAX` | 24 | nodes one directory publish may reach across every key |
| `_DIR_REPUBLISH` | 900 s | between directory sweeps |
| `_DIR_LOOKUP_BUDGET` | 6 s | one directory lookup, whole round; it returns what it has |
